                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "As the developers like to put it, Knight & Damsel is an uncooperative game. Knight & Damsel is essentially a competitive puzzle game. Both of the players (two players required) are trying to be the player that ends up rescuing the other player. To do this players push, climb or destroy the obstacles in their way. Some of the hazards can also be moved onto the path of the other player in order to slow them down. \n\nI had fun with Knight & Damsel. The gameplay is interesting if you have another person to play the game with. The game is easy to pick up so it could work for people that don't play a lot of games. The hazards do tend to create a runaway winner problem though since one hazard leads into another hazard and so on. While fun Knight & Damsel is the type of game that is best in short doses instead of long playthroughs. I think it would work best in 15-30 minute sessions.\n\nKnight & Damsel is also really charming.\n\nIf you don't like these type of puzzle games or don't have anyone to play the game with, I don't think you will enjoy Kinght & Damsel. If you like the theme, have someone else to play with, and the gameplay looks interesting I think you will enjoy Knight & Damsel. \n\nNote: I received a free review copy of the game. Other than the review copy I received no other compensation for this review.\n\n[url=http://www.geekyhobbies.com/knight-damsel-indie-game-review/] Read My Full Review [/url]",
